25b5acc6cbe55f3d2fdcd73ca2b9e252c8fec2bf
SMP: IPI bugfixes and improvements

There are some issues with the current IPI design, among others:
- c_handle_interrupt() only takes the lock if there is no IPI pending.
  It checks the pending hw IRQ instead of clh_is_ipi_pending().
  However, IPI interrupts are async and their arrival can also be
  delayed by other pending, higher priority interrupts. This means
  that by the time we get to c_handle_interrupt(), the hw IPI it
  sees might have been handled long ago and that it proceeds without
  taking the kernel lock, leading to possible race conditions with
  other cores currently running in kernel mode with the lock held.

- NODE_UNLOCK_IF_HELD uses clh_is_self_in_queue() to see whether we
  currently hold the lock. However, that function also returns true
  when we are trying to take the lock, but haven't gotten it yet.
  If it is called when we don't hold the lock, then we will unlock
  the lock for our successor, the core that tries to take the lock
  after us. This would make multiple cores run concurrently. Worse,
  this faulty state persists, as from now on there are two unlocked
  requests globally instead of the intended one.
  The assumption made is that NODE_UNLOCK_IF_HELD is only called
  when we hold the lock. Normally this is true, however, see above.
  There may be other corner cases too, like x86 virtualisation.

- ipiStallCoreCallback() does terrifying things for mysterious reasons.
  The reason is that it can be called at awkward moments, like during
  syscalls or faults, then it can't easily switch to the idle task,
  otherwise the fault or syscall will be attributed to or executed by
  the idle task.

- To handle multiple IPIs in flight, the IPI code got more complicated
  with no real correctness guarantees, e.g. globalsense.

Streamline the IPI code by:
- Making blocking remote call IPIs truly blocking and handle them only
  and immediately in the lock path.
- Only handle IpiRemoteCall_Stall when we are in the interrupt path.
  To achieve this, let remoteTCBStall() return an error if the remote
  core is in the wrong state and restart the syscall that required the
  remote stall. The restart will give the remote core a chance to finish
  what it was doing. Because we have a queueing lock, forward progress
  is guaranteed.

Signed-off-by: Indan Zupancic <indan@nul.nu>

e3625273d49c95af4ca516142e92879734184743
SMP: Make locking code pass GenMC model checking

Use the opportunity to reduce the number of generated synchronisation
instructions (dmb on Arm) to two for lock and one for unlock.

Signed-off-by: Indan Zupancic <indan@nul.nu>

